## Agent Behavior Rules

### ALWAYS Use Worktrees — CRITICAL
<!-- bmad:worktrees-required -->
- **Call `EnterWorktree` at the start of EVERY session before editing any files.** Do not wait for the user to tell you about parallel sessions — you will not always know. The cost of an unnecessary worktree is near zero; the cost of a collision (silent overwrites, lost edits) is high.
- The ONLY exception: the user explicitly tells you "you're the only session, skip worktree."
- When spawning sub-agents via the `Agent` tool for work that edits files, always set `isolation: "worktree"` so each agent gets its own copy of the repo.
- The user has authorized worktree usage as project policy — you do not need an explicit per-session request.

### Branch Naming Convention
<!-- bmad:branch-naming -->
- **Always use descriptive branch names.** Format: `<type>/<short-description>` (e.g., `feat/user-auth`, `fix/null-metadata`, `refactor/format-utils`).
- Types: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `chore/`, `docs/`.
- If `EnterWorktree` auto-generates a name, rename the branch before committing: `git branch -m <new-name>`.

### Worktree Hygiene
<!-- bmad:worktree-hygiene -->
- **Auto-reap runs on every BMAD sync.** `sync-bmad-workflows.sh` invokes the reaper per-project; worktrees whose branch is fully merged AND whose working tree is **completely clean** (no untracked, no modified, no anything) are removed automatically.
- **Manual reap for stale-but-not-strict-clean cases.** The auto-reaper is conservative by design — a previous incident showed lenient reaping can destroy uncommitted in-progress work. Worktrees with stray BMAD sync artifacts in the working tree won't auto-reap. Run `~/bmad-method-v6/sync-bmad-workflows.sh --reap` (all targets) or `--reap <project-path>` (one project) to clean those up; verify each worktree's branch and status first.
- **Never leave a worktree behind after delivering work.** Always clean up via `ExitWorktree` after the PR is merged. The reaper is a safety net, not the primary cleanup mechanism.
- **One worktree per task.** Don't reuse a worktree from a previous task — create fresh from current HEAD.

### Worktree Enforcement Hooks
<!-- bmad:worktree-hooks -->
- **UserPromptSubmit hook** — warns about parallel sessions, suppresses when already in a worktree (`$PWD` contains `/.claude/worktrees/`). Cooldown: emits at most once per 30 minutes per project (marker at `.claude/.parallel-warn-last`).
- **PreToolUse hook (Edit|Write)** — hard-blocks file edits when parallel sessions detected and NOT in a worktree. Allowlisted paths: anything under `/Users/*/bmad-method-v6/` (fork — its own repo) and `_bmad-output/` / `.claude/` (artifact/state dirs that always exist outside worktrees).
- **PreToolUse hook (Bash)** — hard-blocks bash commands that look like edit-equivalents (`sed -i`, `cat > FILE`, `tee FILE`, `awk -i inplace`, `printf/echo > FILE`) under the same conditions as Edit|Write. Targets in `/tmp/`, `/dev/`, fork paths, `.claude/`, and `~/.secrets` are still permitted.
- **After admin-merge with worktree still active:** `gh pr merge --delete-branch` errors locally because the worktree references the branch. The remote merge succeeded; immediately call `ExitWorktree action: "remove" discard_changes: true` to clear the worktree, then fast-forward main from the parent checkout. The local error is harmless; don't chase it as a real failure.
- Hooks count all `claude` processes system-wide (includes other projects). This is intentionally conservative — false positive (forced worktree when unnecessary) is safer than false negative (collision).

### Cross-Repo Edits — when worktree pattern doesn't scale
<!-- bmad:cross-repo-edits -->
- Some tasks legitimately touch multiple repos at once: rolling a BMAD contract change across 13 projects, applying a small refactor to the fork + every consumer, syncing a CLAUDE.md template update everywhere.
- Creating 13 separate worktrees for one script-driven rollout is not the right pattern. Instead:
  - Use **bash-driven multi-repo edits**: a single shell script that loops projects, appends/sed-edits the config, commits, pushes, opens PR, admin-merges.
  - Per-repo commit and per-repo PR (not one giant commit across repos).
  - The bash edit-guard hook allows this implicitly when the edit-equivalents target paths outside the current project worktree.
  - For doc-only / config-only PRs across many repos, `--admin` merge is acceptable per the BMAD deploy contract.
- Fork edits (`~/bmad-method-v6/`) are explicitly allowlisted by the hook — you can Edit/Write fork files directly without a project-worktree gymnastic. The fork is its own git repo with its own delivery flow (`git push myfork custom` after `gh auth setup-git` is configured).

### ALWAYS Deliver Your Work — CRITICAL
<!-- bmad:deliver-work -->
- **Work that isn't merged to `origin/main` doesn't exist.** Never end a session with code sitting on an unmerged branch.
- **SINGLE DELIVERY TRACK — PR to `origin/main`, always. There is no "solo project" local-merge exception.** EVERY implementation workflow (dev-story, quick-dev, design-implement) and every hand edit delivers the same way: **commit → push → PR → merge → deploy**. Skipping a step means the work isn't live.
- **NEVER `git merge` a feature branch into a local `main` you don't push.** That forks local `main` from `origin/main`, and the two diverge silently — the canonical failure is domain/Story work piling up on an unpushed local `main` while UI work ships via PR, producing an N-commits-ahead fork that someone later has to reconcile by hand. `origin/main` is the one source of truth; local `main` only ever moves by fast-forwarding from `origin/main` (`git merge --ff-only origin/main`), never by merging a branch into it.
- In worktrees: use `gh pr view` to verify merge (you can't `git checkout main` from a worktree). Then call `ExitWorktree` with `action: "remove"` to clean up.
- Never push directly to `main` — always a feature branch + PR.
- If the worktree branch name is generic (auto-generated), rename it to `<type>/<short-description>` (`feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`) before pushing.

### Deployment — BMAD contract
<!-- bmad:deploy-contract -->
- Deploys follow the universal BMAD deploy-to-prod contract: `_bmad/bmm/workflows/shared/deployment-to-prod.md`.
- After merging a PR, run `./scripts/bmad-deploy.sh`. Never leave merged code undeployed.
- The script reads `_bmad/bmm/config.yaml` → `deploy:` for project-specific values (platform, build command, dep installer, deploy command, irrelevant-paths filter, success_pattern).
- `npm ci` / `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` / `yarn install --immutable` are pre-authorized by the contract (lockfile-clean — cannot mutate `package.json`). The script auto-runs the configured one when `dep_state_check` is missing.
- BMAD-managed dirty paths (`_bmad/`, `.claude/`, `_bmad-output/`, `docs/`) are filtered as deploy-irrelevant by default; only real code changes block a deploy.
- **Admin-merge on code PRs** is allowed iff the failing required check is structurally unavailable (zero-step quota-exhausted: `conclusion: failure`, `steps: 0`, elapsed < 15s) AND `npm run build` (or equivalent) passed locally. Substantive CI failures never qualify.
- Doc-only / config-only PRs can admin-merge freely and skip the deploy.
- Opt-out: set `deploy.bmad_contract: skip` in `_bmad/bmm/config.yaml` for projects whose deploy doesn't fit the contract (auto-deploy-on-push platforms like Railway/Vercel, multi-step deploys, etc.). The script exits 99 (no-op) and the agent defers to the project's own CLAUDE.md.
- **Posture when `bmad_contract: skip` — NO per-session deploy question.** A skipped contract means deploy is the **owner's deliberate, manual step**, not the agent's. After merging a PR, **state the deploy status as a fact and STOP — do NOT ask "want me to deploy?"**: e.g. *"Merged to `origin/main`; undeployed — deploy is your manual step (see `docs/deployment.md`)."* Deploy ONLY when the owner explicitly says "deploy." This turns a recurring end-of-run question into a one-line status statement. (When the contract is ACTIVE, the rule above already stands — run `./scripts/bmad-deploy.sh` after merge — so neither posture asks.)

### CI Health Check — diagnosis
<!-- bmad:ci-health-check -->
- After pushing/merging: `gh run list --limit 1 --json conclusion,name,createdAt --jq '.[0]'`.
- If the latest run failed in <15s with zero steps → structural failure (GH Actions quota exhausted). The contract's admin-merge carve-out applies.
- If CI failed WITH steps executed → substantive failure. Read the logs and fix; admin-merge is NOT allowed.
- Diagnosis: `gh run view --json jobs --jq '.jobs[0] | {steps: (.steps | length), conclusion}'` — zero steps = structural, non-zero = substantive.

